<!-- ── Why This Module Exists ─────────────────────────────── -->
<h2 id="why-this-module-exists">Why This Module Exists</h2>

<p>An AI hedge fund is, at its core, a pipeline: raw market data flows in, passes through analytical agents, and actionable trading signals come out. But between "raw data" and "trading signal" lies an enormous amount of plumbing that every agent needs but no single agent should own. That is what the Data &amp; Orchestration module provides.</p>

<p>Without this module, every analyst agent would need to independently fetch financial data from external APIs, parse JSON responses into typed objects, handle rate limiting and retries, manage its own cache to avoid redundant API calls, and negotiate shared state with other agents. The result would be duplicated code, inconsistent data, and race conditions everywhere.</p>

<p>Instead, this module centralizes four critical concerns into a clean, layered architecture. The <strong>API layer</strong> (<code>tools/api.py</code>) provides a unified interface to the financialdatasets.ai service, with automatic rate limiting and pagination. The <strong>data layer</strong> (<code>data/models.py</code>, <code>data/cache.py</code>) defines strongly-typed Pydantic models for every financial entity and an in-memory cache with intelligent merge semantics. The <strong>state layer</strong> (<code>graph/state.py</code>) provides the TypedDict that flows through LangGraph's fan-out/fan-in topology, ensuring agents can write concurrently without data loss. The <strong>LLM layer</strong> (<code>llm/models.py</code>) abstracts 13 providers behind a single <code>get_model()</code> factory, so agents never import provider-specific SDKs.</p>

<p>The design philosophy is: <em>agents should think about analysis, not infrastructure</em>. This module is the infrastructure so they don't have to.</p>

<!-- ── Design Decisions ───────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2 id="design-decisions">Design Decisions</h2>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Choice</th><th>Alternatives Considered</th><th>Rationale</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Data validation</td><td>Pydantic models</td><td>Plain dicts, dataclasses, attrs</td><td>Pydantic provides JSON parsing, validation, and serialization in one package — essential when ingesting external API data that may be malformed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cache strategy</td><td>In-memory dict with set-based dedup</td><td>Redis, SQLite, disk-based cache</td><td>The system runs as a single process; in-memory cache eliminates serialization overhead and network latency. Set-based dedup handles overlapping date ranges gracefully</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cache key format</td><td>Composite string <code>f"{ticker}_{start}_{end}"</code></td><td>Hash-based keys, tuple keys</td><td>Human-readable keys simplify debugging. The ticker+date range captures the semantic identity of a query</td></tr>
<tr><td>LLM abstraction</td><td>Factory function with enum</td><td>Abstract base class hierarchy, dependency injection</td><td>A simple <code>get_model(provider)</code> call is sufficient — agents don't need polymorphism, just a callable model instance</td></tr>
<tr><td>State management</td><td>TypedDict with reducer functions</td><td>Pydantic model, class with methods</td><td>LangGraph requires TypedDict with <code>Annotated</code> reducers. <code>operator.add</code> for messages enables append-only message history; <code>merge_dicts</code> for data enables concurrent agent writes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rate limiting</td><td>60s linear backoff, max 3 retries</td><td>Exponential backoff, circuit breaker</td><td>The external API returns 429 with a predictable cooldown period. Linear backoff is simpler and sufficient for a single-process client</td></tr>
<tr><td>Single API provider</td><td>financialdatasets.ai only</td><td>Multi-provider with fallback</td><td>One provider keeps the API layer thin. If a second source is needed, the cache layer already supports key-based routing</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<!-- ── Architecture ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2 id="architecture">Architecture Overview</h2>

<p>The module is organized into four layers. Data flows from the external API through the cache and into typed models, while the state layer orchestrates how agents share information through the LangGraph pipeline.</p>

<div class="diagram-container">
<pre class="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    subgraph External["External Services"]
        API_EXT["financialdatasets.ai API"]
        LLM_EXT["LLM Provider APIs<br/>(OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.)"]
    end

    subgraph Tools["tools/api.py"]
        GP["get_prices()"]
        GFM["get_financial_metrics()"]
        SLI["search_line_items()"]
        GIT["get_insider_trades()"]
        GCN["get_company_news()"]
        GMC["get_market_cap()"]
        PTD["prices_to_df()"]
    end

    subgraph Data["data/"]
        CACHE["cache.py<br/>In-Memory Cache"]
        MODELS["models.py<br/>Pydantic Models"]
    end

    subgraph Graph["graph/state.py"]
        STATE["AgentState TypedDict"]
        MERGE["merge_dicts reducer"]
        OPADD["operator.add reducer"]
    end

    subgraph LLM["llm/models.py"]
        ENUM["ModelProvider Enum"]
        FACTORY["get_model() Factory"]
    end

    subgraph Agents["Analyst Agents"]
        A1["Warren Buffett Agent"]
        A2["Cathie Wood Agent"]
        A3["... 17 more agents"]
    end

    API_EXT -->|JSON| GP & GFM & SLI & GIT & GCN & GMC
    GP & GFM & SLI & GIT & GCN --> CACHE
    CACHE --> MODELS
    MODELS --> STATE
    PTD --> MODELS
    STATE --> MERGE & OPADD
    OPADD -->|messages| Agents
    MERGE -->|data, metadata| Agents
    Agents -->|analysis| STATE
    LLM_EXT --> FACTORY
    ENUM --> FACTORY
    FACTORY --> Agents
</pre>
</div>

<!-- ── Data Flow ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2 id="data-flow">Data Flow</h2>

<p>This sequence traces a complete data-fetch cycle from agent request through cache resolution, external API call, Pydantic validation, and state update.</p>

<div class="diagram-container">
<pre class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    participant Agent as Analyst Agent
    participant API as tools/api.py
    participant Cache as data/cache.py
    participant Ext as financialdatasets.ai
    participant Models as data/models.py
    participant State as graph/state.py
    participant LLM as llm/models.py
    participant Provider as LLM Provider

    Agent->>API: get_prices("AAPL", start, end)
    API->>Cache: lookup("AAPL_2025-01-01_2025-12-31")
    alt Cache Hit
        Cache-->>API: cached Price[] data
    else Cache Miss
        API->>Ext: GET /prices?ticker=AAPL&start=...
        Ext-->>API: JSON response
        API->>Models: parse → Price objects
        Models-->>API: validated Price[]
        API->>Cache: store with dedup merge
    end
    API-->>Agent: list[Price]
    Agent->>LLM: get_model(ModelProvider.OPENAI)
    LLM->>Provider: initialize client
    Provider-->>LLM: model instance
    LLM-->>Agent: ChatModel
    Agent->>State: update data dict
    State->>State: merge_dicts reducer
</pre>
</div>

<!-- ── Narrated Walkthrough ────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2 id="narrated-walkthrough">Narrated Walkthrough: Fetching AAPL Prices</h2>

<p>Let's trace exactly what happens when the Warren Buffett agent calls <code>get_prices("AAPL", "2025-01-01", "2025-12-31")</code> — from the initial function call all the way back to the cached response.</p>

<div class="code-walk">

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Agent invokes the tool.</strong> The Warren Buffett agent, running inside a LangGraph node, calls <code>get_prices("AAPL", "2025-01-01", "2025-12-31")</code>. This function lives in <code>tools/api.py</code> and is registered as a LangGraph tool.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Cache key is constructed.</strong> The function builds the composite key <code>"AAPL_2025-01-01_2025-12-31"</code> by concatenating the ticker and date range. This key uniquely identifies the query.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Cache lookup.</strong> The function checks <code>_cache</code> (a module-level dictionary in <code>data/cache.py</code>) for an existing entry. On the first call, this is a miss.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>HTTP request to external API.</strong> A GET request is sent to <code>https://api.financialdatasets.ai/prices?ticker=AAPL&amp;start_date=2025-01-01&amp;end_date=2025-12-31</code> with the API key in the Authorization header.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Rate limit check.</strong> If the API returns HTTP 429, the function waits 60 seconds and retries, up to 3 times. This linear backoff prevents aggressive polling of the external service.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>JSON parsing.</strong> The raw JSON response is extracted. The <code>prices</code> array within the response body contains daily OHLCV records.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Pydantic validation.</strong> Each JSON record is parsed into a <code>Price</code> Pydantic model, validating that fields like <code>open</code>, <code>high</code>, <code>low</code>, <code>close</code>, and <code>volume</code> are present and correctly typed. Malformed records raise <code>ValidationError</code>.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Cache storage with dedup.</strong> The validated <code>list[Price]</code> is stored in the cache. If a previous entry existed for an overlapping key, the cache uses set-based deduplication on the <code>time</code> field — merging new records with existing ones rather than overwriting.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Return to agent.</strong> The <code>list[Price]</code> is returned to the calling agent. The agent now has typed, validated price data with no awareness of caching or retries.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Agent uses <code>prices_to_df()</code>.</strong> Optionally, the agent calls <code>prices_to_df(prices)</code> to convert the list into a pandas DataFrame for technical analysis. The DataFrame is indexed by date.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Agent queries LLM.</strong> The agent calls <code>get_model(ModelProvider.OPENAI)</code> to obtain a chat model instance, then sends the price data as context for analysis.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Agent writes to state.</strong> The agent produces an <code>AnalystSignal</code> and writes it to the <code>AgentState.data</code> dict. The <code>merge_dicts</code> reducer ensures this write doesn't clobber other agents' data.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Second agent calls <code>get_prices()</code>.</strong> When the Cathie Wood agent requests the same AAPL data, the cache key matches and the data is returned instantly — no API call needed.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
<p><strong>Fan-in aggregation.</strong> After all agents complete, the risk manager reads the accumulated state, which contains every agent's signals merged via <code>merge_dicts</code>, and makes the final portfolio decision.</p>
</div>

</div>

