<tr><td><code>LineItem</code></td><td>data/models.py</td><td>Pydantic Model</td><td>Financial statement line item with name, value, period</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>InsiderTrade</code></td><td>data/models.py</td><td>Pydantic Model</td><td>SEC filing record: owner, transaction type, shares, price</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>CompanyNews</code></td><td>data/models.py</td><td>Pydantic Model</td><td>News article: title, source, date, sentiment</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>AgentState</code></td><td>graph/state.py</td><td>TypedDict</td><td>LangGraph state: messages (append), data (merge), metadata (merge)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ModelProvider</code></td><td>llm/models.py</td><td>Enum</td><td>13-member enum identifying LLM providers</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>get_model()</code></td><td>llm/models.py</td><td>Function</td><td>Factory: takes ModelProvider, returns initialized chat model</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<!-- ── Algorithm Spotlight ─────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2 id="algorithm-spotlight">Algorithm Spotlight</h2>

<h3 id="cache-merge-strategy">1. Cache Merge Strategy</h3>

<p>The cache doesn't simply overwrite on duplicate keys — it <em>merges</em>. When a cache entry already exists for a key, the new data is combined with the old using set-based deduplication on a key field (e.g., <code>"time"</code> for prices). This is critical because overlapping date ranges are common: an agent might request Jan–Jun, then another requests Mar–Dec. The merge ensures no data is lost and no duplicates are created.</p>

<pre><code class="language-python"># Simplified cache merge logic
def cache_set(key: str, new_data: list, dedup_field: str = "time"):
    existing = _cache.get(key, [])
    seen = {getattr(item, dedup_field) for item in existing}
    merged = list(existing)
    for item in new_data:
        if getattr(item, dedup_field) not in seen:
            merged.append(item)
            seen.add(getattr(item, dedup_field))
    _cache[key] = merged</code></pre>

<h3 id="rate-limiting-backoff">2. Rate Limiting with Linear Backoff</h3>

<p>When the external API returns HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests), the client pauses for 60 seconds and retries. This is repeated up to 3 times. Unlike exponential backoff, linear backoff is used because the API's rate window is fixed — waiting longer on subsequent retries doesn't help if the window resets at a constant interval.</p>

<pre><code class="language-python"># Rate limiting pattern
for attempt in range(max_retries):
    response = requests.get(url, headers=headers)
    if response.status_code == 429:
        time.sleep(60)  # Linear backoff: fixed 60s wait
        continue
    response.raise_for_status()
    return response.json()</code></pre>

<h3 id="llm-factory-pattern">3. LLM Factory Pattern</h3>

<p>The <code>get_model()</code> function maps a <code>ModelProvider</code> enum to the corresponding LangChain chat model class. This avoids provider-specific imports in agent code and makes swapping providers a one-line configuration change. The factory handles provider-specific initialization (API keys, base URLs, model names) internally.</p>

<pre><code class="language-python"># Factory pattern
def get_model(provider: ModelProvider, model_name: str = None):
    match provider:
        case ModelProvider.OPENAI:
            return ChatOpenAI(model=model_name or "gpt-4o", ...)
        case ModelProvider.ANTHROPIC:
            return ChatAnthropic(model=model_name or "claude-3-5-sonnet", ...)
        case ModelProvider.GOOGLE:
            return ChatGoogleGenerativeAI(model=model_name or "gemini-2.0-flash", ...)
        # ... 10 more providers</code></pre>

<!-- ── What Could Go Wrong ────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2 id="what-could-go-wrong">What Could Go Wrong</h2>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Impact</th><th>Mitigation</th><th>Severity</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>External API returns 429 after max retries</td><td>Agent receives no data; analysis based on stale or missing information</td><td>Fallback to cached data if available; log warning; agent should handle <code>None</code> returns gracefully</td><td><span class="badge red">High</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Pydantic validation fails on API response</td><td>Data parsing raises <code>ValidationError</code>, blocking the agent</td><td>Wrap parsing in try/except; log malformed records; return partial data if possible</td><td><span class="badge yellow">Medium</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Cache grows unbounded during long runs</td><td>Memory exhaustion on large multi-ticker analyses</td><td>In-memory cache is acceptable for typical runs (dozens of tickers); for production, add LRU eviction or switch to Redis</td><td><span class="badge yellow">Medium</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>LLM provider API key missing or invalid</td><td><code>get_model()</code> raises authentication error</td><td>Validate API keys at startup; provide clear error messages listing which env var to set</td><td><span class="badge red">High</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>State merge conflict on concurrent writes</td><td>Data loss if two agents write the same key</td><td><code>merge_dicts</code> reducer handles this by merging rather than overwriting; agents use unique keys</td><td><span class="badge blue">Low</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<!-- ── Performance Profile ─────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2 id="performance-profile">Performance Profile</h2>

<p>The module's performance characteristics are dominated by two factors: external API latency and LLM inference time.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Typical Latency</th><th>Bottleneck</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Cache hit</td><td>&lt;1ms</td><td>None (dict lookup)</td></tr>
<tr><td>API call (cache miss)</td><td>200–800ms</td><td>Network + external API</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pydantic parsing (100 records)</td><td>&lt;5ms</td><td>CPU</td></tr>
<tr><td>LLM inference</td><td>1–15s</td><td>Provider API</td></tr>
<tr><td>State merge (19 agents)</td><td>&lt;1ms</td><td>None (dict merge)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rate limit backoff</td><td>60s per retry</td><td>External API policy</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The cache is the primary performance optimization: a 10-ticker analysis typically sees 80%+ cache hit rates after the first agent completes, reducing total API calls from ~190 (19 agents × 10 tickers) to ~10.</p>

